      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To fully learn mitosis, we break it down into distinct phases. In prophase, the copied genetic material condenses into visible chromosomes that start to line up. Next, in metaphase, these chromosomes align perfectly down the middle of the cell. Anaphase then pulls the identical chromosomes apart to opposite sides, while telophase completes the process by forming two new nuclei around the separated sets. This ordered division allows organisms to grow and maintain tissue health by making precise copies.",
